{
  "title": "Build Part A - Local Invoice Review POC in GitHub Copilot",
  "locked": false,
  "description": "\n\nThe Build section showcases the best innovation from Microsoft for bringing agents to life. Developers love working with our developer solutions. And with GitHub Copilot App, we’re setting the standard for how developer can rapidly build cutting edge agents that deliver real business impact.\n\nOn-Stage Demo Lead: Jessica Deen\n\nBuild Part A - Local Invoice Review POC in GitHub Copilot\n\nWe begin by building a local proof of concept in GitHub Copilot app, demonstrating how the invoice-review agent takes shape, running with local or simulated data, comparing local and cloud execution, and demonstrating sandbox protection before handing off to Foundry and moving to production.",
  "rows": [
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "You heard what Amy needs. I need to get an agent to production fast to make sure our payments are in line with our contracts, but before I promise anyone anything, I want a proof of concept — something that shows me this can be automated safely and usefully.\n\nI have a lot of choices in where I build, but I start in the GitHub Copilot app. What sets it apart is that it brings agent-driven development natively on GitHub, with the enterprise trust only Microsoft can deliver. It's become the place where I manage everything already in flight, from issues and reviews to my active coding sessions.",
      "demo_actions": "Open the GitHub Copilot app. Briefly orient the viewer to the home screen and top-level navigation. Pause long enough to call out active work, issues, reviews, and automations.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "Let me prove Caldova's idea. One prompt — \"help me check our invoices for overbilling\" — and the app handles the setup.\n\nFirst, it creates an isolated worktree. Think of it as a safe sandbox where I can experiment without affecting the main branch or anyone else's work.",
      "demo_actions": "Move into the working area and enter the prompt to start the proof of concept. Show the setup beginning and briefly point out that a new worktree is being created.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "",
      "narrative": "Then it loads a custom skill I built for local agent development. Unlike a one-off prompt, a skill captures reusable instructions and domain knowledge, so every session starts with the same workflow. I can even commit it to the repo so my teammates get the exact same experience.\n\nAnd before anything runs, it checks the local model with a quick preflight.",
      "demo_actions": "Stay in the setup flow as the session loads the custom invoice-review skill. Briefly open the skill definition or referenced files to show that the workflow comes from reusable project context, not a one-off prompt. Then show the quick local-model preflight check before the agent starts.",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "Here's the app. On the left, the Copilot session driving the work. On the right, the Canvas — a live, interactive view of the agent I'm building.\n\nThis is the part to watch. I'm not just chatting with an agent. I can click inside the Canvas, trigger a step, and the Canvas talks back to the agent. Think of it as a modern, shareable version of localhost development — something I can check into a repo or hand to a teammate.",
      "demo_actions": "Show the app fully loaded. Orient the viewer to the Copilot session on the left and the Canvas on the right. Click into the Canvas, trigger an action, and show that the Canvas sends input back to the agent and updates live.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "And to be clear, this isn't just a model.\n\nIt's an agent: a model, plus instructions, plus context.\n\nRight now it's running entirely on my laptop using a local model. That means lower latency, the ability to work offline, and the assurance that my data never leaves my machine. Even on an airplane, I can keep building and testing.",
      "demo_actions": "Call out that this is an agent made of model, instructions, and context. Keep the local model visible in the current configuration and frame the benefits plainly: on-device response, offline use, and data staying on the laptop.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~40s",
      "narrative": "Now let's give it something real to analyze.\n\nI'll drop in the Aster Ridge invoice.\n\nThe agent immediately gets to work, entirely locally. It identifies a duplicate release fee that was billed twice, and it catches a ten-thousand-dollar mismatch between the line items and the invoice total.\n\nThose are real issues, found on my laptop, with no cloud services involved and no data leaving the room.\n\nThat's the first proof. The invoice review workflow works locally.",
      "demo_actions": "Drop the Aster Ridge invoice into the Canvas and run the invoice review flow locally. Show Ledger identifying the duplicate release fee and the mismatch between line items and the stated total. Keep the moment focused on local execution with no cloud dependency.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "~35s",
      "narrative": "But before I trust this in front of my CFO, I want a second opinion.\n\nSo I ask Copilot to rubber duck the results.\n\nRubber ducking is simply the practice of having something walk through your reasoning and challenge your assumptions. Instead of looking for more answers, it's looking for places where you might be wrong.",
      "demo_actions": "Run /rubberduck on the findings and ask where the agent may be overclaiming. Pause on the response that confirms the math issues but calls out the missing contract context for unit rates and packaging authorization.",
      "screenshot": ""
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "",
      "narrative": "And that's exactly what happens here.\n\nIt agrees that the duplicate charge and the math error are legitimate findings. But it also points out something important: anything that depends on the contract, like negotiated pricing or whether certain packaging was actually authorized, is still an assumption because the agent doesn't have access to the contract.\n\nCalling those overcharges right now would just be guessing.",
      "demo_actions": "",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "",
      "narrative": "That's exactly the kind of feedback I want at this stage. Maybe I want to refine the prompt, update the skill, or add another tool.\n\nAnd because I'm already working inside GitHub Copilot, I can open this entire session directly in Visual Studio Code without losing my context.",
      "demo_actions": "",
      "screenshot": null
    },
    {
      "locked": false,
      "locks": {
        "time": false,
        "narrative": false,
        "demo_actions": false,
        "screenshot": false,
        "visual": false,
        "design_plan": false
      },
      "time": "",
      "narrative": "But eventually this has to move beyond my laptop.\n\nHere's the best part: I don't start over.\n\nIt's the same agent. I'm just promoting it from a local proof of concept into a production-ready environment with access to the contracts it needs.\n\nLet's move it into Microsoft Foundry.",
      "demo_actions": "Set up the handoff to Part 2 by framing this as the same agent and same instructions, now moving to Foundry and adding contract grounding through Foundry IQ.",
      "screenshot": null
    }
  ],
